  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings are based on a small sample of 57 participants in a single, exceptionally green city-state, which may limit generalizability to other urban contexts.",
      "what": "The study analyzed 5,963 momentary observations across 1,225 participant-days from 57 individuals in Singapore, combining geographic ecological momentary assessment (GEMA) with GPS, satellite greenness data, and watch-recorded noise levels. It found that momentary stress peaked when greenness deficits, crowding, and high noise levels coincided, and that longer, uninterrupted stretches of consistent environmental conditions during the day were associated with lower overall daily stress. The relationship between objective and perceived greenness was stronger during active travel than motorized transit, and stronger en-route than after arrival.",
      "why": "It demonstrates that environmental exposure is not just a static spatial measure but a dynamic sequence shaped by travel modes and transitions, showing that even in highly green cities, the mental health benefits of urban nature depend heavily on how transport systems structure daily movement."
    }
  },
